  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on comparing the minimum probabilities of the lotteries and prefers the one with the lower minimum probability (i.e., more risk, in this case Lottery B). However, the individual expresses a clear aversion to accepting lower probabilities (i.e., more risk) unless the monetary gain is substantial. They value the certainty of 3000 Euros and reject increasing risk for only 1000 Euros more. This reasoning opposes the decision reason: the individual is avoiding low probabilities, while the reason favors the lottery with the lowest minimum probability. The individual likely preferred Lottery A due to its certainty, whereas the reason would favor Lottery B due to its 20% minimum probability being lower than A’s 100%. The information considered by the individual (probabilities and trade-offs) overlaps partially with the reason’s domain, but the justification runs counter to the reason’s rule. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used this reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

